{{!
    @template local_moodesk/page_header_action

    One control in a page header's action row.

    Never included directly by a screen — page_header.mustache renders it once
    per action, and the SLOT it is rendered from supplies `variant`. A screen
    that reaches for this partial itself is a screen choosing its own emphasis,
    which is exactly what the component exists to prevent.

    The two forms are spelled out separately rather than sharing a body: a link
    and a button are different elements, and Mustache has no conditional tag
    name. They are four lines each and they sit next to each other, which is a
    better trade than a third partial for the inside of a button.

    An icon-only action keeps its label. It moves from the element's text to its
    accessible name and its tooltip, so nothing is lost to a screen reader or to
    a pointer that hovers — only to the eye, which has the glyph.

    Context variables:
      * label         - Localised label. Visible text, or the accessible name when icononly.
      * url           - Destination. Absent for a button.
      * hasurl        - True to render <a>, false to render a <button>
      * form          - Id of a form on the page the button submits, or absent
      * hasform       - True to render <button type="submit" form="…">, false for type="button"
      * icon          - Sprite id without the `mhd-icon-` prefix
      * hasicon       - True when an icon is present
      * icononly      - True to hide the label and use it as the accessible name
      * variant       - Button colour variant: 'filled' (primary) or 'outlined' (secondary),
                        or their danger counterparts 'danger' / 'outlined-danger'
      * attributes    - [{name, value}] extra HTML attributes (data-action, target, rel)
      * hasattributes - True when there are extra attributes

    Example context (json):
    {
      "label": "New department",
      "url": "/local/moodesk/department_edit.php",
      "hasurl": true,
      "icon": "plus",
      "hasicon": true,
      "icononly": true,
      "variant": "filled",
      "attributes": [],
      "hasattributes": false
    }
}}
{{#hasurl}}
<a href="{{url}}"
   class="mhd-btn mhd-btn--{{variant}}{{#icononly}} mhd-btn--icon{{/icononly}}"
   {{#icononly}}aria-label="{{label}}" title="{{label}}"{{/icononly}}
   {{#attributes}}{{name}}="{{value}}" {{/attributes}}>
  {{#hasicon}}
  <svg class="mhd-btn__icon" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
    <use href="#mhd-icon-{{icon}}"></use>
  </svg>
  {{/hasicon}}
  {{^icononly}}{{label}}{{/icononly}}
</a>
{{/hasurl}}
{{^hasurl}}
{{! A button. `type="button"` for the JS-driven kind; `type="submit"` pointing at
    a form elsewhere on the page — by id, through the HTML `form` attribute — for
    a state change the screen spelled out as a <form> in its own template. The
    header carries the control, the screen keeps the request. }}
<button {{#hasform}}type="submit" form="{{form}}"{{/hasform}}{{^hasform}}type="button"{{/hasform}}
        class="mhd-btn mhd-btn--{{variant}}{{#icononly}} mhd-btn--icon{{/icononly}}"
        {{#icononly}}aria-label="{{label}}" title="{{label}}"{{/icononly}}
        {{#attributes}}{{name}}="{{value}}" {{/attributes}}>
  {{#hasicon}}
  <svg class="mhd-btn__icon" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
    <use href="#mhd-icon-{{icon}}"></use>
  </svg>
  {{/hasicon}}
  {{^icononly}}{{label}}{{/icononly}}
</button>
{{/hasurl}}
